Change per passage (what the representation did):
   1   2.5  ~~
          Socrates depicts chained cave-prisoners who can perceive only shadows and echoes,
          establishing them as like ourselves in enlightenment.
   2 12.75  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates shows that release from illusion begins with painful glare, confusion, and
          resistance, as the prisoner clings to familiar shadows before being dazzled by greater
          realities up to the sun.
   3 12.75  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates traces the freed prisoner's gradual ascent from shadows and reflections to
          the sun itself as cause of the visible world, leaving him to pity his former den and
          despise its shadow-honours.
   4 47.25  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates shows the returning philosopher blinded, ridiculed, and threatened with
          death, then explicitly decodes the cave as the visible world and crowns the ascent
          with the Idea of the Good.
   5  30.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates excuses the enlightened philosopher's unwillingness to return and ridiculous
          blindness in human affairs, then teaches that dazzlement comes from either entering
          light or darkness and should be diagnosed rather than mocked.
   6  20.5  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates rejects teachers who claim to insert knowledge into the soul, arguing that
          education is instead the art of turning the soul's innate power from becoming toward
          being and the Good.
   7  18.5  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates argues that keen natures freed from sensual drag could see truth sharply, so
          the founders must compel those who reach the Good to descend again and govern.
   8  20.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates answers Glaucon's charge of injustice by arguing that educated philosophers
          owe ruling service to the whole State, whose reluctant rulers will govern best.
   9  19.0  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
          Socrates concludes that just philosophic pupils will accept ruling as a stern
          necessity, and only rulers given a better life of philosophy and rich in virtue can
          keep the State well-ordered.
Constructed meaning c03_1 (passage 4, inferred): Returning to the cave with vision attuned to the sun renders the freed prisoner blind in darkness, ridiculous in shadow-judging, and liable to be killed for attempting liberation.
    built from: Returnee blinded by darkness (provides the physiological readjustment that
    disables the returnee in darkness); Ridiculed shadow competitor (provides the social
    interpretation of that failure as ridiculousness proving ascent worthless); Death threat to
    liberator (provides the escalation from mockery to lethal hostility toward anyone attempting
    liberation)
Constructed meaning c03_2 (passage 4, inferred): The cave journey from chains through fire and sun maps onto the soul's ascent from the visible world of shadows to knowledge of the Good as universal author of beauty, right, light, and truth.
    built from: Cave to intellect mapping (provides the explicit decoding frame prison as
    visible world, fire as sun, ascent as soul to intellect); Good as ultimate source (provides
    the crowning culmination in the Idea of the Good as universal author seen last with effort);
    Seeing Sun Itself (provides the penultimate story vision now reread as prefiguring vision of
    the Good); Chained Underground Prisoners (provides the starting story condition now reread
    as life in the visible world taking shadows for reality)
Constructed meaning c04_1 (passage 5, inferred): Blind confusion from going into light and from coming out of light are opposite natural dazzles that call not for mockery but for diagnosing direction, pitying the still-dazzled ascender and counting the darkness-blinded descender happy.
    built from: Two kinds of blindness (provides the general principle of two opposite causes of
    visual bewilderment for eye and mind); Retreat from glaring light (provides the ascending
    instance of dazzle when forced to look into light); Returnee blinded by darkness (provides
    the descending instance of dazzle when thrust back into darkness); Suspend mockery and
    diagnose (provides the prescription to suspend laughter and first diagnose direction of
    travel); Happy descent versus pity (provides the divergent verdicts of happiness for the
    descender and pity for the ascender)
Constructed meaning c04_2 (passage 5, inferred): The soul that has seen the sun is unwilling to descend because return means painful blindness in darkness and contention over worthless images of justice.
    built from: Philosopher unwilling to descend (provides the phenomenon of unwillingness to
    descend to human affairs after beatific vision); Returner's courtroom blindness (provides
    the cost of descent as blindness and ridicule when disputing about mere images of justice);
    Contempt for Shadow Honours (provides the worthlessness of what is returned to, rejected in
    favor of enduring anything else)
Constructed meaning c05_1 (passage 6, inferred): The soul's ascent from visible prison to the Good occurs not by implanting new sight but by the art of turning the whole soul's already-present power from becoming toward being.
    built from: Cave to intellect mapping (provides the ascent frame from prison to intellect
    that is being specified); Good as ultimate source (provides the endpoint of ascent as the
    Good); Innate power of learning (provides the pre-existing capacity that is turned rather
    than created); Whole-soul turning toward being (specifies the turn as movement of the whole
    soul from becoming to being toward the Good); Art of easy conversion (defines education as
    the art of reorienting existing sight rather than implanting sight); Freed Faculty Sees
    Truth (shows the same keen faculty would see truth keenly if turned upward in youth); Leaden
    Weights of Pleasure (identifies sensual pleasures as leaden drag to be severed for upward
    turning)
Constructed meaning c05_2 (passage 6, inferred): Keen intellect without conversion retains its power but puts it in evil service, so whether wisdom helps or harms depends on the direction of its turn rather than its sharpness.
    built from: Clever rogue's misdirected sight (provides concrete example that keenness
    persists while serving evil); Innate power of learning (provides principle that learning
    capacity already exists even when misdirected); Art of easy conversion (provides remedy as
    reorientation of wrongly turned sight rather than increase of power)
Constructed meaning c06_1 (passage 7, inferred): Those who would rather remain above in blessed contemplation must still be compelled to descend and share the prisoners' labours because neither the ignorant nor perpetual recluses can govern.
    built from: Philosopher unwilling to descend (establishes the enlightened reluctance to
    descend and longing to dwell above); Compelled Descent to Govern (establishes the founders'
    order to descend and share labours and honours); Neither Extreme Can Rule (provides the
    political reason that neither the ignorant nor endless learners can be able ministers);
    Islands of the Blest (illustrates the recluse extreme who fancies blessed isolation and
    refuses to act)
Constructed meaning c07_1 (passage 8, inferred): Compelling the educated philosophers to descend and care for others is not unjust because they were bred to rule the hive for the happiness of the whole state rather than any one class.
    built from: Compelling Philosophers Is Just (states the normative conclusion that compulsion
    is not unjust); Happiness Of Whole State (supplies the whole-state purpose that justifies
    the compulsion); Philosophers As Hive Rulers (supplies the bred-for-purpose debt that
    obligates ruling service)
Constructed meaning c07_2 (passage 8, inferred): The philosophers' unwillingness to descend marks the very reluctance that makes rulers best, so their compelled descent produces the most quietly governed state.
    built from: Philosopher unwilling to descend (provides the unwillingness to descend to human
    affairs); Reluctant Rulers Govern Best (revalues reluctance to govern as the mark of the
    best rule); Compelled Descent to Govern (provides the compulsion that harnesses reluctance
    into shared rule)
Constructed meaning c08_1 (passage 9, inferred): Those who long to dwell above will nonetheless accept turns at ruling as a stern necessity because they are granted a shared heavenly life for the greater part of their time.
    built from: Philosopher unwilling to descend (provides the initial reluctance and longing to
    dwell above that must be overcome); Compelled Descent to Govern (provides the external
    requirement to descend and share labours and honours); Pupils' Shared Heavenly Life
    (provides the compensation of shared heavenly life that makes refusal unlikely and turns
    descent into rotation); Ruling as Stern Necessity (provides the internalized acceptance of
    office as necessity distinguishing just pupils)
Constructed meaning c08_2 (passage 9, inferred): A well-ordered State requires rulers given a better life of true philosophy, rich in virtue and wisdom, which alone lets them despise political ambition and avoid ruinous fighting over office.
    built from: Better Life For Rulers (states the requirement of a better life than ruling for
    a well-ordered State); Riches In Virtue Wisdom (specifies that better life consists in
    virtue and wisdom rather than wealth); Philosophy Above Political Ambition (identifies that
    life as true philosophy which alone despises political ambition); Office Fighting Ruins
    State (provides the ruinous consequence avoided when rulers do not seek private advantage
